data.content:
## What happened

In C# 14 / .NET 10, **new span overload resolution rules** cause `array.Contains(num)` to bind to `MemoryExtensions.Contains<T>(ReadOnlySpan<T>, T)` instead of `Enumerable.Contains<T>(IEnumerable<T>, T)`.

`MemoryExtensions.Contains` is **not representable in expression trees**, so when the expression is compiled (especially with `preferInterpretation: true`), it throws at runtime — the expression tree compiler cannot handle the `Span`-based method.

## How to fix

Force the call to bind to `Enumerable.Contains` by disambiguating:

```csharp
// Option 1: Cast to IEnumerable<T>
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => ((IEnumerable<int>)array).Contains(num);

// Option 2: Use .AsEnumerable()
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => array.AsEnumerable().Contains(num);

// Option 3: Call Enumerable.Contains explicitly
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => Enumerable.Contains(array, num);
```

All three prevent the compiler from selecting the `MemoryExtensions` span overload and keep the expression tree valid.
